To solve this problem, you need to implement a **Global Tooltip Provider**.

Since Next.js components are isolated, you cannot rely on local state (like `useState` inside the component) to track which tooltip is open. Instead, you will create a Context that lives at the top of your application (usually in `_app.tsx` or a layout wrapper) and pass the "open/close" state down to every tooltip instance.

Here is a complete, copy-pasteable solution.

### 1. Create the Context and Hook

First, create a file to manage the global state. This will hold the list of currently open tooltips.

`src/context/TooltipContext.tsx`

```tsx
'use client'; // Required for Next.js App Router

import React, { createContext, useContext, useState, useCallback, ReactNode } from 'react';

// Define the shape of our data
interface TooltipState {
  isOpen: boolean;
  content: string;
  position: { x: number; y: number };
}

interface TooltipContextType {
  openTooltip: (content: string, position: { x: number; y: number }) => void;
  closeTooltip: () => void;
  isOpen: boolean;
  content: string;
  position: { x: number; y: number };
}

const TooltipContext = createContext<TooltipContextType | undefined>(undefined);

export const TooltipProvider: React.FC<{ children: ReactNode }> = ({ children }) => {
  const [state, setState] = useState<TooltipState>({
    isOpen: false,
    content: '',
    position: { x: 0, y: 0 },
  });

  const openTooltip = useCallback((content: string, position: { x: number; y: number }) => {
    setState({
      isOpen: true,
      content,
      position,
    });
  }, []);

  const closeTooltip = useCallback(() => {
    setState({
      isOpen: false,
      content: '',
      position: { x: 0, y: 0 },
    });
  }, []);

  return (
    <TooltipContext.Provider value={{ openTooltip, closeTooltip, isOpen, content, position }}>
      {children}
    </TooltipContext.Provider>
  );
};

export const useTooltip = () => {
  const context = useContext(TooltipContext);
  if (!context) {
    throw new Error('useTooltip must be used within a TooltipProvider');
  }
  return context;
};
```

### 2. Wrap Your Application

You must wrap your entire application in the `TooltipProvider`. In the App Router, this is typically done in `src/app/layout.tsx`.

`src/app/layout.tsx`

```tsx
import type { Metadata } from "next";
import { Geist, Geist_Mono } from "next/font/google";
import "./globals.css";
import { TooltipProvider } from "@/context/TooltipContext";

const geistSans = Geist({
  variable: "--font-geist-sans",
  subsets: ["latin"],
});

const geistMono = Geist_Mono({
  variable: "--font-geist-mono",
  subsets: ["latin"],
});

export const metadata: Metadata = {
  title: "Create Next App",
  description: "Generated by create next app",
};

export default function RootLayout({
  children,
}: Readonly<{
  children: React.ReactNode;
}>) {
  return (
    <html lang="en">
      <body className={`${geistSans.variable} ${geistMono.variable}`}>
        {/* This wraps the whole app so all components can access the context */}
        <TooltipProvider>
          {children}
        </TooltipProvider>
      </body>
    </html>
  );
}
```

